    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>Keyboard path</strong>: there is no
    "Enter to edit" — every cell is a real control, so Tab (or a click)
    focuses it and it's immediately editable; commit is the native
    <code>change</code> (blur for text, immediate for select/checkbox),
    Save/Cancel are the next Tab stops in the row, and inside a composite
    widget Escape returns focus to the cell when
    <code>data-grid-nav</code> is in play. Everything composes unchanged:
    <code>bo:cell-change</code>, dirty tracking, <code>bo:row-save</code>,
    live mode — this is a <em>skin</em> on the same machinery, not a mode.
    <strong>Two channels</strong>: the hover/focus reveal is the visible
    affordance; the always-real controls are the programmatic one (a
    screen reader announces "edit text", "combo box" at rest exactly as in
    the chromed version).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Save-per-change — <code>data-row-edit="live"</code></h2>
    <p>The second save model: no Save/Cancel buttons, every committed
    change (blur for text, immediate for select/checkbox/tag) dispatches
    <code>bo:row-save</code> at once and re-baselines — wire the listener
    to a <code>fetch</code>/<code>hx-patch</code> and edits persist as
    they happen. Same markup minus the actions cell. <strong>Trade-off to
    know</strong>: this commits per <em>field</em> (a half-typed new line
    hits your server validation mid-row); many real ERPs commit per
    row-exit instead — if that's your model, keep batch mode and trigger
    Save from your own row-blur logic. Optimistic-update/rollback on a
    failed PATCH is your code's job either way; this demo only logs.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
